WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Intestinal contents normally move along the lumen.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.910
Here, the moving contraction
advances material through an open

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:07.326
segment.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.716
A mechanical obstruction places
a lesion in that route.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.293
Incoming contents collect upstream.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.170
Watch the proximal segment
expand while material fails to

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.247
pass the narrowed region.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.387
Chapter forty-one explains that
a lower small-bowel obstruction can

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.210
produce more distention because
a greater length of bowel

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:25.912
lies upstream.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.404
Paralytic ileus is different.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.311
The packet describes failure
of motility, especially after surgery,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.020
without an obstructing lesion.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.392
The lumen remains open, but the propulsive wave stops.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.080
The animation compares these
mechanisms schematically; its dimensions do

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.687
not represent a patient or a clinical threshold.
